[00:00.000 --> 00:06.560]  Yesterday I posted a video that got a lot of attention because it shows there's a focal point of stability
[00:06.560 --> 00:11.840]  between a big ring magnet and a floater magnet that lies above it. Today I'm going to
[00:11.840 --> 00:15.120]  explain the physics and show you how you can do these same experiments at home.
[00:16.480 --> 00:22.560]  There's a ring magnet of 140 millimeters with an 80 millimeter hole. There we have a 70 millimeter
[00:22.560 --> 00:27.840]  neodymium magnet and they're all North Pole magnets and you can see the 70 millimeter magnet is
[00:27.840 --> 00:33.360]  repelled from the central hole but it's actually attracted to all the outer parts of the magnet.
[00:34.480 --> 00:43.120]  Repel attracted. So we'll explore more today how that works and I'll show you the physics and you can
[00:43.120 --> 00:47.200]  repeat the same experiments for yourself. You won't find this anywhere in textbooks. They don't
[00:47.200 --> 00:52.880]  know anything about it. This is where you have to go to learn about magnetism. As a follow-up to
[00:52.880 --> 00:59.120]  a previous video we're going to ask what does it take to create a focal point of stability
[01:00.080 --> 01:05.280]  for a little magnet floating over a ring magnet. Here we have a fair right ring magnet
[01:05.280 --> 01:12.400]  a size of 145 millimeters with an 80 millimeter hole in front of it we put a 40 millimeter magnet
[01:12.400 --> 01:16.640]  and you can see it's repelled but also it attracts back inward.
[01:17.600 --> 01:26.800]  So that's what we call the focal point. Let's try a series of magnets of larger size to see next
[01:26.800 --> 01:32.080]  what will happen. When we go up to a magnet of diameter 50 millimeters we see almost the same
[01:32.080 --> 01:39.120]  behavior. It repels but also it gets attracted inward. A magnet of 60 millimeter diameter
[01:39.120 --> 01:45.760]  shows the same effect. It's repelled from the center of that ring magnet but also it's pulled inward
[01:45.840 --> 01:50.320]  toward the focal point. Now we'll go up to a magnet of 70 millimeters in diameter
[01:50.320 --> 01:56.080]  which is just slightly smaller than the 80 millimeter hole. Again it's repelled very strongly
[01:56.080 --> 02:02.000]  and also it's pulled inward very strongly. We'll look at a magnet of 80 millimeters which is the
[02:02.000 --> 02:07.360]  same size as the hole in this fair right ring magnet. It repelled very strongly from the center
[02:07.440 --> 02:12.480]  but also it's pulled inward. Repel pulled inward.
