[00:00.000 --> 00:04.240]  Here's something interesting. If we take a gyroscope and unbalance it slightly by
[00:04.240 --> 00:09.600]  putting some blue tackle to one side, without any spin on the gyroscope disc, it
[00:09.600 --> 00:13.840]  will just, blue tackle sit at the bottom. But if we spin the wheel very strongly
[00:13.840 --> 00:19.440]  tortoise, then you'll see the disc will stay erect and it will process
[00:19.440 --> 00:22.640]  anti-clockwise.
[00:23.360 --> 00:28.800]  Alternatively, if we spin the disc away from us, it will stay erect and the disc will
[00:28.800 --> 00:33.360]  go clockwise like that.
[00:36.160 --> 00:41.200]  Finally, if we spin the disc away from us like that, and we speed it up just a
[00:41.200 --> 00:51.680]  little bit by touching it, with some resistance, the disc will rise up to vertical.
[00:52.320 --> 01:00.880]  Or if we spin the disc tortoise and push it on harder, the disc will rise up
[01:00.880 --> 01:06.160]  to vertical. So it's clearly pushing against the vacuum of space by gyroscopic
[01:06.160 --> 01:11.200]  inertia, and as long as it's spinning, the heavy part of the gyroscope will remain
[01:11.200 --> 01:21.520]  erect, and then if it stops spinning, the blue tackle will fall down again.
[01:22.480 --> 01:26.880]  Like that. It's some very interesting laws which you never
[01:26.880 --> 01:33.200]  studied before. Toward you, it goes anti-clockwise,
[01:34.160 --> 01:39.600]  away from you, it goes clockwise. I have no idea why that is. If somebody can explain
[01:39.600 --> 01:46.080]  that to me, I would be grateful. Thank you very much to the stars.
